TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Image 1; Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.3 Orbit","issue":"The claimed revisit and nadir-mosaic periods divide ground-track spacing (km) by reachable width or swath (km), producing a dimensionless ratio rather than days. A repeat-ground-track or time-dependent coverage analysis is needed to support these periods."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The daily plan budgets 60,000 km² of imaging, while the reef area is stated as 344,400 km². Covering the entire reef within five days would require at least 68,880 km²/day even with no overlap; the coverage and data-volume plan are not reconciled."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The 50 Mbit/s X-band rate and resulting capacity are assumed rather than verified with a link budget. The report acknowledges this, so achievable downlink capacity remains unestablished."}],"overall":"Strong, well-structured preliminary mission report with clear users, orbit design, STK access results and EOL planning. The main weakness is that the key revisit and complete-mapping claims are not supported by valid time-based coverage calculations; data-volume and link feasibility also need further work. Weighted result: 69/100.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Clearly explains the mission, its Australian environmental need, the choice of a single spacecraft, and specific operator and customer groups. The rationale is detailed and supported with citations. A few claims rely on relatively general or secondary sources, preventing a top mark.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used. This also simplifies communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS), which already runs the long-term monitoring program for the reef"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The design is comprehensive and uses STK outputs, calculations, orbital elements, access data and an EOL analysis to address the major requirements. However, the claimed five-day revisit and 45-day nadir mosaic derive days by dividing track spacing in km by a footprint width in km; this is dimensionless and does not establish revisit time without ground-track drift or coverage analysis. The daily imaging plan also does not reconcile with full-reef coverage, and the assumed downlink rate lacks a link budget.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"With J2 = 1.0826 × 10⁻³, R = 6,378 km, a = 6,978 km and mean motion n = √(μ/a³) = 1.0831 × 10⁻³ rad/s:"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"STK 11 was used to calculate when the satellite is above the horizon at Mount Pleasant."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"A full link budget for the X-band downlink has not been calculated in this report and is the main item of further work for this segment."},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The EOL plan is therefore passive: a drag sail is deployed at the end of the mission."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion draws specific results from the design and candidly identifies limitations and next steps. It is appropriate and useful, although some of its coverage conclusions repeat the unsupported revisit calculations.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations. First, Vega C has not yet flown, so the Falcon 9 rideshare service is kept as a fallback"},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"With these points addressed in the next design phase, the mission can provide the direct, regular bleaching maps that current temperature-based monitoring cannot."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a clear engineering structure, executive summary, numbered sections, labelled tables and figures, and a substantial bibliography with in-text citations. It is readable and generally consistent. Some prose and reference entries could be more polished, and the central coverage presentation is not adequately substantiated, which limits confidence in the presentation of results.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report presents the design of ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), a single Earth observation microsatellite for monitoring coral bleaching on the Great Barrier Reef."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Table 5: STK access report, ZYZZsat to Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory (elevation above 0°)."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[11] D. A. Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications, 4th ed. Hawthorne, CA, USA: Microcosm Press, 2013."}]}]}
